# 60-Days-to-Cloud

Prework_Foundations Day 1: How AI Agents Are Changing the Software Development Lifecycle Software used to be built by people handing documents to each other, one stage after another. In the AI-native version, agents do most of the work. Humans set the direction, encode the rules, and own the high-risk decisions.

The six stages: before and after
1. Plan
Before: Requirements came from committees, workshops, and sign-offs, then someone wrote them up by hand.

After: AI reads the raw sources (tickets, customer feedback, support logs) and pulls out the pain points into a single  intent.md  file that people can read and agents can act on.

2. Design
Before: Analysts wrote a spec and designers interpreted it, and details got lost in the handoff.

After: Requirements and design happen in one working session with an agent. Team standards are encoded as reusable “skills” and versioned in git.

3. Build
Before: Code and tests were written by hand, and documentation came last, if it came at all.

After: AI generates code and tests. Team knowledge lives in versioned, machine-readable files like  CLAUDE.md , so it doesn’t leave when people do.

4. Test
Before: QA acted as a gate at the end of each stage.

After: Evaluations run continuously during implementation, so problems are caught while building.

5. Deploy
Before: Humans reviewed every line, and governance happened in review cycles that weren’t always consistent.

After: Agents run layered reviews, and humans focus on regulated and critical code. Governance is enforced as the agent acts, with hooks serving as automatic approval gates.

6. Maintain
Before: Humans watched production for bugs.

After: Agents monitor live deployments. When a control threshold is breached, the agent diagnoses the issue and writes it back as a new  intent.md , which starts the cycle again.

My key takeaway
The biggest change is the shape of the process. The old SDLC is a straight line, and the AI-native one is a loop. Production feedback flows straight back into planning.
Humans aren’t removed. Their role moves from doing every step to:
#defining intent

#encoding standards and guardrails

#reviewing what’s critical, regulated, or high-risk


